WEBVTT

00:00.180 --> 00:01.410
Instructor: Alright, so let's start

00:01.410 --> 00:02.820
with the obvious question.

00:02.820 --> 00:06.129
What exactly is GitHub or what does it do?

00:06.129 --> 00:08.520
There's many answers to that question

00:08.520 --> 00:09.690
because GitHub over the years

00:09.690 --> 00:12.780
has really evolved and added on a bunch of features.

00:12.780 --> 00:14.280
But the core mechanic,

00:14.280 --> 00:16.950
the single thing that GitHub is known for

00:16.950 --> 00:20.970
is that it's a hosting platform for Git repositories.

00:20.970 --> 00:24.480
GitHub allows us to put our Git repos in the cloud.

00:24.480 --> 00:25.800
We can access them from anywhere,

00:25.800 --> 00:27.360
we can share them with people,

00:27.360 --> 00:30.930
and we can collaborate with people on Git repos.

00:30.930 --> 00:32.850
So GitHub has a bunch of features built

00:32.850 --> 00:34.191
that have to do with collaboration

00:34.191 --> 00:37.290
and discussion about Git repos.

00:37.290 --> 00:39.030
So I showed this slide very early on

00:39.030 --> 00:40.819
but I just want, I return to it.

00:40.819 --> 00:42.630
There is an important

00:42.630 --> 00:44.850
huge difference between Git and GitHub.

00:44.850 --> 00:46.917
Git, hopefully you're familiar with by now

00:46.917 --> 00:49.218
is a version control system

00:49.218 --> 00:51.734
that we run on our machines locally.

00:51.734 --> 00:54.150
We don't need the internet, we don't need an account.

00:54.150 --> 00:56.190
You could go your entire life working with Git

00:56.190 --> 00:57.420
and never using GitHub.

00:57.420 --> 01:00.450
GitHub on the other hand, is a website.

01:00.450 --> 01:03.180
It's a hosting platform for Git repositories.

01:03.180 --> 01:04.980
You need the internet to access it.

01:04.980 --> 01:06.205
You need an account.

01:06.205 --> 01:09.870
It is completely, I don't wanna say completely separate

01:09.870 --> 01:14.280
from Git, because it is a hosting service for Git repos,

01:14.280 --> 01:16.889
but Git itself is completely independent.

01:16.889 --> 01:19.800
So imagine I'm working on a video game,

01:19.800 --> 01:22.658
a mobile game that I'm developing on my machine,

01:22.658 --> 01:25.020
and I'm using Git to track it.

01:25.020 --> 01:26.160
I have a couple of commits.

01:26.160 --> 01:27.780
Let's say there's like a couple hundred.

01:27.780 --> 01:30.060
Those three you're seeing on my machine are just

01:30.060 --> 01:31.740
to fit in the diagram

01:31.740 --> 01:33.960
and I can make a GitHub account,

01:33.960 --> 01:38.100
make a GitHub repository, and push my local code,

01:38.100 --> 01:40.860
that repo from my machine up to GitHub.

01:40.860 --> 01:43.620
And I could do this for a couple of reasons.

01:43.620 --> 01:46.080
One, I just wanna back things up.

01:46.080 --> 01:48.030
So I keep doing my work

01:48.030 --> 01:50.220
and I can push those changes up to GitHub.

01:50.220 --> 01:54.330
So now GitHub has a record of my repository.

01:54.330 --> 01:57.210
So maybe there's a house fire, a horrible tragedy.

01:57.210 --> 01:59.820
I get a new laptop, I lost my old laptop

01:59.820 --> 02:01.170
with the repo on it.

02:01.170 --> 02:02.430
Well, fortunately for me,

02:02.430 --> 02:04.860
assuming I kept my GitHub up to date,

02:04.860 --> 02:07.500
I can go to GitHub and get that code

02:07.500 --> 02:10.350
and pull it down to my machine.

02:10.350 --> 02:12.330
So that's kind of a, I won't say rare,

02:12.330 --> 02:14.490
but not as common scenario.

02:14.490 --> 02:18.210
What really is great about pushing my code up to GitHub

02:18.210 --> 02:20.100
is that I can collaborate.

02:20.100 --> 02:22.950
So maybe I decide I need to bring in, you know, a

02:22.950 --> 02:25.687
I don't know, another engineer over here on the left

02:25.687 --> 02:29.100
and I don't know what her name is, let's say Zelda.

02:29.100 --> 02:32.100
And then I bring in a graphics artist,

02:32.100 --> 02:33.960
a pixel artist over here on the right.

02:33.960 --> 02:35.610
His name is Link.

02:35.610 --> 02:36.837
So I need their help.

02:36.837 --> 02:40.020
So using GitHub, they can all clone

02:40.020 --> 02:42.150
or they can all get access to this repository

02:42.150 --> 02:43.143
on their machine,

02:44.607 --> 02:45.440
and then they can make contributions,

02:45.440 --> 02:46.440
put 'em up on GitHub

02:46.440 --> 02:48.030
and then we can pull them down

02:48.030 --> 02:49.948
and get those latest contributions to those new commits.

02:49.948 --> 02:51.879
And this allows us to collaborate

02:51.879 --> 02:54.720
and maybe eventually if my game's a hit,

02:54.720 --> 02:57.900
we're talking about dozens or hundreds of collaborators.

02:57.900 --> 03:00.033
So this is really where GitHub shines.

03:01.337 --> 03:02.170
It's important to know that GitHub

03:02.170 --> 03:04.556
is not the only option for some of these features,

03:04.556 --> 03:07.860
specifically for hosting repositories.

03:07.860 --> 03:10.588
There are other options like GitLab, Bitbucket,

03:10.588 --> 03:13.560
but the truth is that in recent years

03:13.560 --> 03:16.830
GitHub has just completely obliterated the competition.

03:16.830 --> 03:19.050
It's now become the world's largest host

03:19.050 --> 03:20.700
of source code.

03:20.700 --> 03:22.380
In early 2020,

03:22.380 --> 03:25.087
they reported having over 40 million users,

03:25.087 --> 03:30.087
these days it looks like over 56 million in 2021,

03:30.660 --> 03:32.580
just from their own marketing page,

03:32.580 --> 03:33.633
which we'll come back to.

03:33.633 --> 03:36.030
It's really a lovely marketing page.

03:36.030 --> 03:38.790
And the last slide here on GitHub that's very important

03:38.790 --> 03:41.040
is that it's free for our purposes.

03:41.040 --> 03:43.770
So unlike a lot of subscription platforms

03:43.770 --> 03:47.190
or websites where the free tier is just garbage,

03:47.190 --> 03:49.350
or it's just supposed to like lure you

03:49.350 --> 03:51.120
in to paying more, right?

03:51.120 --> 03:53.940
Where maybe you have a very limited set of features

03:53.940 --> 03:55.350
and if you pay 10 bucks a month

03:55.350 --> 03:57.375
then you can actually use this in a useful way.

03:57.375 --> 03:59.550
That is not GitHub's model.

03:59.550 --> 04:01.630
GitHub offers paid tiers,

04:01.630 --> 04:04.560
but they're largely oriented towards enterprise,

04:04.560 --> 04:05.790
towards companies.

04:05.790 --> 04:08.430
For individuals, for developers,

04:08.430 --> 04:10.530
the free tier gives you pretty much everything

04:10.530 --> 04:11.363
you would need

04:11.363 --> 04:14.430
unless you are creating some incredibly popular tool,

04:14.430 --> 04:16.478
an open source project that's like used

04:16.478 --> 04:18.060
by millions of people.

04:18.060 --> 04:21.480
In all likelihood, you'll never need to subscribe to GitHub.

04:21.480 --> 04:24.242
So I can show you the different pricing tiers here, right?

04:24.242 --> 04:27.761
Here's free, there is a team, there's enterprise level,

04:27.761 --> 04:29.137
but with this free level,

04:29.137 --> 04:33.540
we have unlimited public and private repositories,

04:33.540 --> 04:35.055
unlimited collaborators.

04:35.055 --> 04:37.410
There's these things called GitHub actions

04:37.410 --> 04:38.340
we'll talk more about.

04:38.340 --> 04:40.050
So there are some distinctions there.

04:40.050 --> 04:43.650
You pay for more actions or more minutes a month.

04:43.650 --> 04:45.330
And then there are some security features.

04:45.330 --> 04:47.190
Larger enterprise companies might want,

04:47.190 --> 04:49.241
single sign-on, advanced auditing,

04:49.241 --> 04:50.942
but we really don't need to worry about that.

04:50.942 --> 04:54.300
We're focused on just the core mechanics of GitHub.

04:54.300 --> 04:55.620
It's all here for us,

04:55.620 --> 04:57.630
which we are very, very thankful for.

04:57.630 --> 05:00.360
Somewhat rare in the world of subscriptions.

05:00.360 --> 05:03.180
So I'll end off by just scrolling through their

05:03.180 --> 05:04.770
beautiful marketing page.

05:04.770 --> 05:06.729
I really love the illustrations they have.

05:06.729 --> 05:09.450
You can see, you know, some stats about

05:09.450 --> 05:10.860
how many people are using it.

05:10.860 --> 05:13.020
72% of Fortune 50 companies.

05:13.020 --> 05:15.840
It's really grown both in market share for just

05:15.840 --> 05:18.960
open source development and random developers like me,

05:18.960 --> 05:21.420
but also in large companies.

05:21.420 --> 05:22.964
So tons of big companies use it.

05:22.964 --> 05:25.320
I'd say it all boils down to this,

05:25.320 --> 05:28.110
give your code a home in the cloud.

05:28.110 --> 05:30.395
But there are many other features.

05:30.395 --> 05:33.690
It almost acts as a social network for Git repos

05:33.690 --> 05:36.720
where you can chat, write comments,

05:36.720 --> 05:38.310
approve, disapprove,

05:38.310 --> 05:41.100
suggest changes to projects,

05:41.100 --> 05:44.010
merge code in, reject code,

05:44.010 --> 05:46.650
call attention to certain lines of code.

05:46.650 --> 05:48.930
Then there are some other things that don't necessarily

05:48.930 --> 05:50.790
have to do with Git repos,

05:50.790 --> 05:52.500
but are kind of adjacent,

05:52.500 --> 05:54.715
like these interactive developer environments.

05:54.715 --> 05:55.860
I think, what do they call it?

05:55.860 --> 05:56.700
Code spaces.

05:56.700 --> 05:57.930
That's new.

05:57.930 --> 06:00.750
GitHub actions, we'll come back to later on.

06:00.750 --> 06:01.989
Additional features like

06:01.989 --> 06:04.380
automatically scanning your repositories

06:04.380 --> 06:06.480
and looking for common vulnerabilities

06:06.480 --> 06:09.270
and letting you know ahead of time

06:09.270 --> 06:10.830
and a whole bunch of other things.

06:10.830 --> 06:12.840
So, ah, let's end with that.

06:12.840 --> 06:14.580
What a beautiful illustration.

06:14.580 --> 06:16.230
Okay, so that's it.

06:16.230 --> 06:18.660
GitHub does a lot, but for us, what we care about

06:18.660 --> 06:20.940
is the fact that it will host our repositories

06:20.940 --> 06:22.641
and help facilitate collaboration.

06:22.641 --> 06:23.703
Whoof.
